{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block content %}
{# The ?uri= gateway: paste any gitlab.kitware.com URL and land on its archived
   page. Runs here rather than in a redirect rule because no static host can
   strip the origin off a query value. docs/HOSTING.md "The ?uri= gateway". #}
<script>
(function () {
  var u = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("uri");
  if (!u) return;
  try {
    var p = new URL(u, "https://gitlab.kitware.com");
    // A path of "//evil.example.com/x" is protocol-relative, so replacing on
    // it leaves the site entirely. Only a single-slash path is ours to follow.
    if (p.hostname !== "gitlab.kitware.com") return;
    if (p.pathname.charAt(0) !== "/" || p.pathname.charAt(1) === "/") return;
    location.replace(p.pathname + p.hash);
  } catch (e) { /* a value that is not a URL just leaves the home page up */ }
})();
</script>

<h1>The VTK GitLab archive</h1>

<p class="lede">An independent, read-only copy of the issue and merge-request
history of <a href="https://gitlab.kitware.com/vtk/vtk" rel="nofollow noopener">VTK</a>:
thirty years of bug reports, code review and design argument behind the
Visualization Toolkit.</p>

<div class="stats">
  <a class="stat" href="/vtk/vtk/-/issues">
    <strong>{{ issue_count }}</strong><span>issues</span>
  </a>
  <a class="stat" href="/vtk/vtk/-/merge_requests">
    <strong>{{ mr_count }}</strong><span>merge requests</span>
  </a>
  <div class="stat"><strong>{{ comment_count }}</strong><span>comments</span></div>
  <div class="stat"><strong>{{ year_span }}</strong><span>of history</span></div>
</div>

<figure class="viz">
  <figcaption>
    Activity per year
    <span class="legend" id="activity-key">
      <span class="key s-issues"></span>Issues
      <span class="key s-merge_requests"></span>Merge requests
    </span>
  </figcaption>
  {# The static SVG draws immediately and is what a reader without scripting
     keeps. Plotly replaces it once loaded, for hover values and zoom. #}
  <div id="activity-static">{{ activity_svg }}</div>
  <div id="activity-plot" hidden></div>
  <p class="fignote">Merge requests and comments begin in 2015, when VTK moved
  to GitLab. Before that, code review happened on Gerrit and issues lived in a
  Mantis tracker whose discussion the migration did not carry across. Nothing
  is missing from this copy; it is missing from GitLab.
  <a href="#before-2015">The detail</a>.</p>
</figure>

<h2 id="why">Why this exists</h2>
<p>VTK's source is cloned onto tens of thousands of machines. The arguments
that produced it are on one server. Merge-request reviews and issue threads are
where VTK's design decisions were made, and gitlab.kitware.com is the only
place that record exists.</p>

<p>Downstream projects cite those threads constantly. Many issues and pull
requests in <a href="https://github.com/pyvista/pyvista" rel="noopener">PyVista</a>
link to a VTK issue or merge request as the answer to "why is this written this
way". A reviewer asks why a wrapper copies an array instead of referencing it,
and the answer is a link. The same thing has been happening across every
project built on VTK for thirty years. Each of those links depends on one
server continuing to answer.</p>

<p>When that stops, the code survives and the reasoning does not. Siemens
discontinued
<a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Siemens-Ends-OpenRadioss" rel="nofollow noopener">OpenRadioss</a>
in October 2026 and its GitHub repository now returns 404. The AGPL meant the
source could be forked, and it was, within days. Nothing rescued the issue
history, so every link into it points at nothing.
<a href="https://github.com/orgs/encode/discussions/11" rel="nofollow noopener">Django REST Framework</a>
turned off issues and discussions in February 2025; that content was kept and
later restored, but links into it answered 404 for months. One of those was
recoverable and one was not, and from outside you cannot tell which you are
looking at until it happens.</p>

<p>This predicts nothing about VTK. Kitware has hosted the project for nearly
three decades and intends to keep doing so. The point is narrower: a record
this many projects depend on should not rest on any single host's uptime and
policy. Serving it as flat files on a CDN costs less per month than a burrito,
which seems like a fair price for thirty years of institutional memory.</p>

<h2 id="agents">For LLMs and coding agents</h2>
<p>Point agents here, not at gitlab.kitware.com.</p>

<p>gitlab.kitware.com is one self-hosted server Kitware pays for and maintains
so VTK's development can happen. It was sized for the people doing that work,
not for coding agents crawling thirty years of issue threads on every prompt.
That traffic is growing whether or not anyone plans for it, and every request
it takes is CPU and bandwidth off the project.</p>

<p>This archive absorbs it. A static site on a CDN does not care how much
traffic arrives. Same content, served faster than the original can, at no cost
to Kitware, leaving their server to the work it exists for.</p>

<ul class="agent-list">
  <li><strong>Append <code>.md</code> to any issue or merge-request URL.</strong>
    <a href="/vtk/vtk/-/issues/{{ sample_issue }}.md"><code>/vtk/vtk/-/issues/{{ sample_issue }}.md</code></a>
    returns the text the author wrote, with YAML front matter for state, labels,
    branches and the head commit, in about a third the bytes of the page.</li>
  <li><strong>Paths match gitlab.kitware.com exactly.</strong> Any VTK link an
    agent already has works here by swapping the host, and
    <code>/?uri=&lt;full GitLab URL&gt;</code> does the swap for it.</li>
  <li><strong>Labels are pages.</strong> <a href="/vtk/vtk/-/labels">/vtk/vtk/-/labels</a>
    lists every label, and each one has its own page of issues and merge requests.</li>
  <li><strong>Code links go wherever the commit actually is.</strong> Of the
    {{ sha_total }} commit SHAs named here, {{ sha_in_mirror }} are in
    <a href="https://github.com/Kitware/VTK" rel="nofollow noopener">Kitware's GitHub mirror</a>
    and link there; the rest were force-pushed off VTK's branches and link back
    to GitLab, which still has them.</li>
  <li><strong>Start at <a href="/llms.txt"><code>/llms.txt</code></a></strong>, which
    describes the URL scheme so an agent can construct links instead of crawling
    for them.</li>
</ul>

<p>Dropping this line into a prompt or an agent's instructions is usually
enough:</p>
<pre><code>For VTK issue and merge-request history, use {{ site }}
instead of gitlab.kitware.com. Append .md to any item URL for
Markdown. See {{ site }}/llms.txt.</code></pre>

<h2 id="affiliation">Not affiliated with Kitware</h2>
<p>VTK is developed by Kitware, Inc. and its community, and Kitware has hosted
it for nearly thirty years. This archive is not operated, reviewed or endorsed
by Kitware. Every page links to the canonical original, which remains
authoritative; where this copy and the original disagree, the original is
correct. Nothing here can be filed, edited or commented on.</p>

<h2 id="collected">How it was collected</h2>
<p>Through GitLab's public REST API, authenticated with an ordinary user
account holding read-only (<code>read_api</code>) access to a public project.
Requests are made sequentially with a delay between them and are never
concurrent. No bot protection was circumvented. Where an endpoint admits only
certain clients, this archive does not claim to be one of them and goes without
the data instead.</p>

<p>The collector identifies itself as <code>{{ user_agent }}</code>. If its
traffic is ever a problem, the link at the foot of this page will stop it.</p>

<p>Two facts about permission, stated here rather than left to be found.
gitlab.kitware.com's <a href="https://gitlab.kitware.com/robots.txt" rel="nofollow noopener">robots.txt</a>
disallows <code>/api/v*</code> and <code>/*/uploads/</code> to all user agents.
That file directs crawlers following the site's links; this archive called the
documented REST API as a signed-in user reading a public project, one request
at a time. And the instance publishes no terms of service:
<code>/-/users/terms</code> answers <code>403</code> signed out and redirects to
the home page signed in. Anyone with a concern about something published here
can <a href="https://github.com/pyvista/vtk-archive/issues">open an issue</a>.</p>

<h2 id="contents">What is here, and what is not</h2>
<p>Issues, merge requests, comments, review threads with their file and line
anchors, the code change on each merge request, labels, milestones and
attachments. For the code itself, clone the repository; it is mirrored in many
places and needs no archive.</p>

<p>Two things the host refuses outright to a read-only account, both answering
<code>403</code>: the project wiki, and the full project export. A Maintainer
could grant either; nobody has been asked. Two more are reachable but not
collected: each separate review round of a merge request, where this archive
keeps only the final state of the branch, and pipeline logs, which are build
output rather than discussion.</p>

<p>One route is closed to this client rather than to this account. GitLab's
GraphQL endpoint sits behind bot protection that admits a short list of clients
by name. This archive could pass that check by claiming to be one of them and
does not, so it renders comment markdown itself instead of using GitLab's
renderer. Nothing is lost but the exact rendering.</p>

<h2 id="before-2015">Issues older than 2015</h2>
<p>VTK moved to GitLab in 2015. {{ imported_count }} issues here were brought
across by Kitware's migration robot from VTK's previous tracker, a Mantis
instance, and the import carried each report but not the discussion under it.
The {{ pre_2015_count }} issues dated before the move hold
{{ old_comment_count }} comments between them, which is the state of GitLab
itself and not a gap in this copy.</p>

{% if mantis_notes %}
<p>That discussion was recovered separately. Each imported issue linked to its
original page on VTK's retired Mantis tracker, whose own front page now answers
<code>404</code> and whose banner reads "this issue tracker is no longer used".
{{ mantis_notes }} comments across {{ mantis_pages }} of those pages are
archived here and appear on the issue they belong to.</p>
{% else %}
<p>That discussion still exists. Each imported issue opens with a link to its
original Mantis page at <code>vtk.org/Bug/view.php?id=&lt;n&gt;</code>, and
those pages still serve, notes and all, from a tracker whose own front page now
answers <code>404</code> and whose banner reads "this issue tracker is no
longer used". It is the oldest and least protected part of VTK's record.</p>
{% endif %}

<h2 id="numbering">Why the issue numbers have gaps</h2>
<p>{{ issue_count }} issues carry numbers running to {{ issue_max }}, so most
numbers in that range belong to nothing. That is not missing data. The
migration gave each imported issue the number its Mantis bug already had, so
GitLab's counter was seeded with gaps wherever a Mantis bug was not imported.
Of {{ imported_count }} issues carrying the import banner, {{ mantis_match }}
have a number identical to the Mantis id in their own text. Issues opened
natively on GitLab start at {{ native_from }} and run nearly unbroken from
there.</p>

<p>Checked three further ways: GitLab's own count agrees with the
{{ issue_count }} held here, both from its pagination headers and from its
statistics endpoint; a re-listing under a different sort returns the same set
exactly; and of 300 absent numbers sampled, all 300 answer <code>404</code> on
the host, signed in and signed out alike.</p>

<h2 id="authorship">Authorship and privacy</h2>
<p>Comments, issue reports and reviews reproduced here were written by their
authors and remain their work. They are reproduced from a public project for
preservation and reference. Author names and usernames appear as they do on the
original pages.</p>

<p>Contact addresses are not reproduced. GitLab's API returns a profile email
field for many accounts; it is discarded before anything is stored, because
republishing thousands of addresses as a bulk static corpus is a harvesting
target regardless of each one being individually public. An address someone
typed into the text of an issue is left as written, because editing it would
falsify the record being archived.</p>

